e new  details until the very end  in fact we don't even learn the villains  end goal until the penultimate scene it  makes it really easy to follow the  conspiracy but it undermines the pacing  and honestly kills a lot of the drama  because important revelations just  aren't given enough time to sink in  let's run down the plot so we're all on  the same page in short John Tillman is a  fighter in a video game called Slayers  that uses death row convicts as the  avatars for players if a convict  survives 30 rounds they win their  freedom and at the start of the film  Tillman aka cable is on his 27th match  as the plot unfolds we learned the  castle the inventor of Slayers is up to  no good and plans to use the nano brain  technology to take over the world  Tilman knows this because he was one of  the first  subjects and castle used the tech to  make him murder the other test subject  which is why he was thrown in jail but  these are all kind of suppressed  memories that don't come out in detail  until Ludacris gives him a brain scan at  the end of the second act tilman manages  to break out of the game he rescues his  wife and daughter confronts castle they  fight Tillman wins people cheer they  shut down the mind-control network  Tillman and family drive off into the  unknown credits roll there's also  subplots involving a reporter trying to  get the scoop of her career a teenage  player controlling Tillman in the game  and a band of terrorists slash freedom  fighters called the human  with a Zed led by Ludacris most of them  died okay why would I say that has  little to do with video games well  because the video games in this context  are used only as a loose framing device  for a mystery story that's about control  only the outward trappings and facades  of video games are used the lingo and  the user interfaces but the actual  mechanics and broader social  implications of video games are missing  in the end the games depicted in gamer  are less video game and more reality TV  and sport first let's look at society  the other game castle invented where  players control real people to see how  this use of video games is little more  than a skin-deep reference it's  presented as a social game reminiscent  of Second Life  there was society the ultimate sim  environment where players don't control  virtual animated characters but actual  living breathing human beings they  walked them they talked them they juiced  him they rocked him the problem with  society is that it's actually a really  crappy game with tons of drawbacks and  almost no unique advantages first of all  players aren't controlling these games  with matrix-style brain plugs they're  not physically experiencing the events  they're playing the game on monitors  with voice commands and motion controls  meaning there's still that  depersonalized layer in the way of the  experience you're not actually living  life as a super hot woman for a time  you're just controlling an avatar that  looks like a superhot woman and  pretending to be them the lack of direct  stimulus is actually really important  because even with the other drawbacks  and limitations there would still be an  obvious appeal in the you could  experience drugs crazy sex or other  exotic experiences with little to no  personal inconvenience or repercussions  you'd be able to get blindingly drunk  than sober up the moment you  disconnected or experienced the rush of  jumping off something really high  without being the one to live with a  broken leg but that's not how it works  in society if you make your avatar get  drunk then all you've really done is  watch someone get drunk so let's talk  about the big downside there are  of limitations in society your avatar is  a real human being meaning if they get  injured sick or go home  they're unplayable you're also limited  to playing a human you can only make use  of the costumes that the actor has  available and you're limited to looking  like one of the actors also you're still  limited to the rules of reality you  can't run forever jump super high  breathe underwater fly a spaceship or  use magic plus you still have to deal  with scarcity if there's no room in the  bar you can't go in if all the cars are  taken you can't drive around if all the  arcade games are being played you have  to wait in line in contrast Second Life  allows you to play as a bipedal unicorn  a vampire a giant rabbit a robot or even  on airship the world uses simplified  rules that take out most of the  bothersome limitations of reality your  character doesn't become unplayable for  a year if you jump off a balcony and  break their legs they don't die if you  fail to feed them and they can teleport  from one side of the world to the other  he'll at the push of a button they can  fly there's still some obvious appeal in  society vicarious gratification  anonymous and idealized social  interaction reduce consequences for  abnormal or antisocial behavior you know  so on and so forth but none of these are  unique to society in fact the only thing  that's unique to society is the  knowledge that you're watching real  people the implication here is that the  main appeal of society isn't  role-playing and escapism its misery  tourism and custom pornography tons of  people would still sign up for that but  it's not a very good game slayers fares  a little better in comparison but still  falls victim to a lot of the same  problems the game wouldn't actually be  all that much fun because it's  incredibly inconvenient  you can only play during scheduled  events and if your Slayer dies you're  out of the match until the next match  the movie isn't explicit about the time  between matches but it seems to be at  least a day or two and during the time  between matches you have no apparent  opportunity to practice so it's going to  take forever to get to be any good at  the game there's a really odd moment in  the first scene with Simon the gamer in  control of Tilman we're a couple of  twins colum  and offer him money in exchange for a  turn playing his cable cable instant  transfer to the account of your choice  he was not for sale thank you this makes  almost no sense cable is a celebrity  okay but I don't get why they would want  to play his cable when the stakes are so  high because the game would be so hard  to get good at that just get cable  killed they wouldn't be semi famous  groupies they'd be the bimbos that got  an international celebrity killed  because they suck at the game and he is  an international celebrity the opening  credits go to great lengths to show just  how popular Slayers is and when Cable  wins his 28th match huge crowds of  people celebrate from Los Angeles to  Beijing considering the stakes involved  in being successful at a game that's  this popular in reality it would be  dominated by elite teams of players and  not apparently masses of unorganized  scrubs blindly charging at each other  since we're already going in that  direction it's worth pointing out that  the actual structure of Slayers is kind  of jacked up it's a team game sort of  it's hard to tell because it's  impossible to tell players apart from  each other they aren't color-coded into  teams or anything even on Simon's  user-interface but people occasionally  work together so it's not a complete  free-for-all alright I know that this is  starting to sound particularly nitpicky  but I think there's some good ideas in  here that could be salvaged for a better  movie basically if you want to include  video games as a central element of your  movies world then those games need to  behave right they need to feel right and  they need to work if they're not  believable then they break the  audience's immersion and diffuse tension  this is another part of why I said that  gamer isn't worth watching more than  once I know I go out of my way to  overthink this stuff but I can't be the  only person who watched this and kept  asking w